{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3060","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-runtime-hints-r7-regroom.md@sha256:7231abf227bc9de733920f5cb89a1424a95b63ffe758aa6f795fbbe5f4d5e3ef"},"owner_reference":"3060","content":"# Architecture Spec: R7-Compliant Runtime Hints in `backlog_view` (re-groom, 2026-09-20)\n\nBacklog item: #3060 \u2014 \"fix: runtime hints steer agents away from paginated navigation\"\n\n**Supersedes**: `plan/architect-runtime-hints-r7.md` (2026-08-20) for implementation purposes. That\ndocument's \u00a72 ordinal-compatibility resolution and \u00a73/\u00a74 diffs for Acceptance Criteria 1, 2 and 4\n(entry 13.3) remain correct and unchanged \u2014 verified against current `origin/main`\n(`d547db370`, 2026-09-20: `_build_compact_manifest`/`_build_over_budget_view` are byte-identical to\nwhat that spec diffs against, confirming the fix is still unmerged). This document does not\nre-derive that work; it recaps it in \u00a72 and adds the three pieces the 2026-09-19 re-groom found\nmissing from both the prior spec and the drafted PR #3649: the AC3 budget-limit wording, the AC5\nschema-description fix, and the AC8 branch/merge strategy. \u00a75 gives the complete, consolidated diff\ntarget (all of AC1/2/3/4/5) so an implementer has one document to build against.\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nAcceptance Criteria (item #3060, entry 13.3, the only non-struck version) now has 8 criteria. Five\nare behavioral changes inside `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`:\n\n| AC | What | Status before this document |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `_hint` leads with section-narrowing, not `summary=False` | Designed (prior spec \u00a73), drafted on PR #3649 |\n| 2 | `_usage` for a narrowed-and-still-over-budget request names `map=True`, not repeated `sections=` advice | Designed (prior spec \u00a74), drafted on PR #3649 |\n| 3 | That same `_usage` states `map=True` is not unconditionally safe on a large item | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a73).** |\n| 4 | Item bookkeeping records R7 prohibition 3 as unmet for the narrowed hint, pending #3059 | Already true (Dependencies 8.2) \u2014 no code change, no design gap |\n| 5 | `summary` Field description lists a section-scoped read before an unscoped full read | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a74).** |\n| 6 | `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s ownership table keeps #3060 listed until 1-5, 7, 8 hold | Already true today (row: \"Consumer guidance and contract identity\" names #3060) \u2014 no design work, a close-out check only |\n| 7 | Five named test files exit 0 | Test plan in \u00a76 |\n| 8 | The change is on `origin/main`, not only an open PR | **Not designed. Closed here (\u00a77).** \u2014 PR #3649 is `CONFLICTING` and, per the table above, incomplete against AC3/AC5 regardless |\n\nAll of it stays inside `server.py`. No change to `disclosure_handler.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, or\n`content_normalizer.py` \u2014 confirmed again this pass, no new evidence changes that boundary.\n\n## 2. Recap: Ordinal-Compatibility Resolution (unchanged from prior spec)\n\nThe prior spec's \u00a72 answered whether `_build_over_budget_view()`'s narrowed `_usage` may name\n`navigate=<N>` using the `[N]` numbers in its own `sections_index`. Answer: no \u2014 that field is\nbuilt by `_sections_index_from_result()`'s `enumerate(result.sections.items())` fallback\n(server.py:2133), a **dict**-order enumeration, while `navigate=` ordinals are resolved by\n`OrdinalPathMapper` against a **document-order** `[N]`-line parse\n(`ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()`, whose own docstring states the two collapse differently\nunder duplicate section titles). Naming an unverified ordinal would violate R7 prohibition 3\n(\"never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies\") a second time in the same fix.\nThe resolution: name `map=True` instead \u2014 it takes no address argument, so nothing about it can be\ninvalid. This is unchanged and still the correct mechanism for AC2. See the prior artifact's \u00a72 for\nthe full four-finding evidence chain; it was not re-verified line-by-line this pass because no fact\nin it depends on anything the 2026-09-19 re-groom touched (AC3/AC5/AC8 are additive, not revisions\nof AC1/2/4's mechanism).\n\n## 3. AC3 \u2014 The `map=True` Budget-Limit Statement\n\n### 3.1 What AC3 requires\n\n> The same `_usage` text that recommends `map=True` also states, in its own wording, that a\n> `map=True` call is not guaranteed to stay within the response token budget on a large item \u2014 the\n> hint does not present `map=True` as an unconditionally safe next step, it names the limit.\n\nOwner decision (Dependencies 8.2, RT-ICA Final 2.7 condition 11): the limit is recorded \"in the\n`_usage` hint text itself\" \u2014 not a separate response field, not deferred to #3059.\n\n### 3.2 Resolving Q1 (wording and whether to name #3059)\n\nTwo design choices were open per the re-groom (Q1): word the limit so it is both observable to a\ntest and non-steering, and decide whether to name issue #3059 or name an in-response recourse.\n\n**Decision: name the MAP response's own `over_budget` field, not the issue number.** An agent\nconsuming this hint cannot act on \"wait for #3059 to land\" \u2014 an internal tracker reference is not\nan address or a mechanism it can call, so naming it would add prose with no operational value (and\narguably repeats the R7 prohibition-1 shape this whole fix removes: a suggestion the caller can't\nact on now). What the agent *can* act on: `map=True`'s own response carries an `over_budget`\nboolean (`MapResponse.over_budget`, `disclosure_handler.py:387-403`, confirmed present and\ndiagnostic-only, unchanged by this fix per Anti-Goal 2). Naming that field gives the agent an\nobservable signal to check after making the call \u2014 consistent with the \"what went wrong, what to\nexpect\" shape of actionable tool-response guidance generally (no MCP-specific `backlog_view`\nprecedent for this exact wording exists yet in the codebase; the general principle \u2014 a hint states\nan observable follow-up check, not an untraceable external reference \u2014 is what both R7's existing\ntext and general MCP tool-response guidance agree on, per this pass's supporting web search, cited\nin Research Source Notes below).\n\nAlso resolves Fact-Check gap #1 from the re-groom (`_usage` narrowed text on PR #3649 has no such\nstatement at all) \u2014 this document is the first to specify the wording.\n\n### 3.3 Wording (verbatim, becomes part of `_build_narrowed_usage()` in \u00a75)\n\n```text\nThis response exceeded the 4000-token budget (99999 chars in full form), even after narrowing\nto a single section \u2014 further section= narrowing is not available for this request. Get this\nitem's ordinal map, then page through the oversized section with navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\n  backlog_view(selector='#3060', map=True)\nNote: on a large item, that map=True call can itself exceed this budget (check its own\nover_budget field) \u2014 there is no smaller retrieval for this section available yet.\n```\n\nThe added sentence: (a) is present unconditionally in the narrowed branch, so it can never be read\nas \"safe in this case\" \u2014 it always accompanies the `map=True` suggestion; (b) names one\nalready-real field (`over_budget`, part of the MAP response shape today) rather than a value this\nfix would have to invent; (c) makes no claim about *when* a smaller retrieval will exist, only that\nnone does yet \u2014 accurate per AC4/Dependencies 8.2 (pending #3059) without naming #3059 by number.\n\n## 4. AC5 \u2014 `summary` Parameter Schema Description\n\n### 4.1 What AC5 requires\n\n> The `backlog_view` tool's `summary` parameter description ... lists a section-scoped read before\n> an unscoped full-content read \u2014 not full-content first.\n\n### 4.2 Current text (`server.py:2264-2274`, confirmed unchanged on `origin/main` this pass)\n\n```python\nsummary: Annotated[\n    bool,\n    Field(\n        description=(\n            \"When True (default), returns a compact routing manifest with issue_number, title, labels, \"\n            \"status, plan_address, sections_index (all available sections as [N] Title (count) lines), \"\n            \"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load full content or specific sections. \"\n            \"When False, returns the full response unchanged.\"\n        )\n    ),\n] = True,\n```\n\n### 4.3 New text \u2014 one-clause reorder, nothing else changes\n\n```python\nsummary: Annotated[\n    bool,\n    Field(\n        description=(\n            \"When True (default), returns a compact routing manifest with issue_number, title, labels, \"\n            \"status, plan_address, sections_index (all available sections as [N] Title (count) lines), \"\n            \"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load specific sections or full content. \"\n            \"When False, returns the full response unchanged.\"\n        )\n    ),\n] = True,\n```\n\nOnly `\"load full content or specific sections\"` \u2192 `\"load specific sections or full content\"`. No\nmention of `navigate=`/`map=` is added here, for the identical reason \u00a73.3 of the prior spec gives\nfor not adding it to `_hint`: this description's own `sections_index` reference carries the same\ndict-order-vs-document-order ambiguity (\u00a72), and this field never claims an ordinal is valid, so\nadding one here would introduce a claim the description can't back up.\n\n### 4.4 Verified safe \u2014 no pinned test on this string\n\n`grep -rn \"full content or specific sections\" plugins/development-harness` (this pass, 2026-09-20)\nfinds exactly one occurrence: the `server.py:2270` line itself. No test asserts this description\nstring verbatim \u2014 Acceptance Criterion 4/7's named test list (`test_backlog_view_summary_true_*`,\n`test_build_over_budget_view_*`, `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS`) covers response *field* shape, not the\ntool schema's `Field(description=...)` text. This edit has no test-breakage risk to track.\n\n## 5. Consolidated Diff Target (AC1 + AC2 + AC3 + AC4 bookkeeping + AC5, all in one place)\n\n### 5.1 `_build_compact_manifest()` \u2014 `_hint` (AC1, from prior spec \u00a73, unchanged)\n\n```python\n\"_hint\": (\n    f\"Load specific sections: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='<index, title, or /regex/>')\\n\"\n    f\"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)\"\n),\n```\n\n### 5.2 `_build_over_budget_view()` \u2014 signature (AC2, from prior spec \u00a74.1, unchanged)\n\n```python\ndef _build_over_budget_view(\n    result: _models.ViewItemResult,\n    full_chars: int,\n    selector: str,\n    *,\n    narrowed_to_single_section: bool = False,\n) -> dict[str, object]:\n```\n\nKeyword-only, defaults `False` \u2014 every existing 3-positional-argument call site (including both\ncited unit tests) is unaffected.\n\n### 5.3 Call-site signal computation (AC2, from prior spec \u00a74.2, unchanged)\n\nInside `backlog_view()`, replacing the current unconditional call at `server.py:2513`:\n\n```python\nserialised = _json.dumps(full_response)\nif _view_payload_token_count(full_response) > _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET:\n    narrowed_sections = full_response.get(\"sections\")\n    narrowed_to_single_section = bool(\n        (section is not None or sections_filter is not None)\n        and isinstance(narrowed_sections, dict)\n        and len(narrowed_sections) == 1\n    )\n    return _respond(\n        BacklogViewResponse,\n        _build_over_budget_view(\n            result, len(serialised), selector, narrowed_to_single_section=narrowed_to_single_section\n        ),\n        exclude_none=False,\n        exclude_unset=True,\n    )\n```\n\nUses `full_response[\"sections\"]` (post-`_filter_view_sections()`), not `result.sections` \u2014 the\nplural `sections=[...]` path mutates `full_response` in place but not `result`\n(`_filter_view_sections()` docstring, server.py:1240-1249).\n\n### 5.4 `_build_over_budget_view()` \u2014 `_usage` (AC2 + AC3, updated this pass)\n\n```python\n\"_usage\": (\n    _build_narrowed_usage(selector, full_chars)\n    if narrowed_to_single_section\n    else (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form). \"\n        \"Use the sections_index below to identify which sections you need, \"\n        \"then request them individually:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, sections=['Section Name'])\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='0,1,3')\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='/regex/')\"\n    )\n),\n```\n\n```python\ndef _build_narrowed_usage(selector: str, full_chars: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Build _usage for an over-budget response already narrowed to one section.\n\n    Section-based narrowing (section=/sections=) is already exhausted for the\n    single section that remains \u2014 repeating that advice would violate R7's\n    \"never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted\" prohibition.\n    Names map=True instead: it takes no address argument, so it cannot itself\n    violate R7's \"never name a mechanism absent from the response it\n    accompanies\" prohibition \u2014 see architect-runtime-hints-r7.md \u00a72 for why a\n    navigate=<ordinal> suggestion here would be unverified.\n\n    States the map=True budget limit unconditionally (AC3): map=True is never\n    presented as guaranteed-safe, and the recourse named (the MAP response's\n    own over_budget field) is something the codebase can already show the\n    caller today, not a forward reference to unshipped work (#3059).\n\n    Args:\n        selector: Original selector string used to build the usage hint.\n        full_chars: Character length of the serialised full response.\n\n    Returns:\n        Usage string naming map=True as the next call, with its budget caveat.\n    \"\"\"\n    return (\n        f\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \"\n        f\"({full_chars} chars in full form), even after narrowing to a single section \u2014 \"\n        \"further section= narrowing is not available for this request. \"\n        \"Get this item's ordinal map, then page through the oversized section with \"\n        \"navigate=/head=/skip_tokens=:\\n\"\n        f\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', map=True)\\n\"\n        \"Note: on a large item, that map=True call can itself exceed this budget \"\n        \"(check its own over_budget field) \u2014 there is no smaller retrieval for this \"\n        \"section available yet.\"\n    )\n```\n\n(As in the prior spec: splitting the narrowed branch into its own function is a readability\nchoice, not a requirement. An inline conditional producing the same string content is equally\nacceptable.)\n\n### 5.5 `summary` Field description (AC5, new this pass \u2014 \u00a74.3 above, repeated here for diff completeness)\n\n```python\n\"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load specific sections or full content. \"\n```\nreplaces\n```python\n\"_full_chars, and _hint showing how to load full content or specific sections. \"\n```\nat `server.py:2270`, no other line in that `Field(...)` block changes.\n\n### 5.6 Substring/field preservation (verifies AC7's named test list stays green)\n\n- `_hint`: `\"summary=False\"` still present (now second line); `{selector}` still present verbatim\n  in both lines \u2014 preserves `test_backlog_view_summary_true_returns_compact_manifest` and\n  `test_backlog_view_summary_true_hint_contains_selector`.\n- Un-narrowed `_usage` branch: byte-identical to current text \u2014 preserves\n  `test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param`.\n- Neither builder adds/removes a top-level key in either branch \u2014 preserves\n  `test_build_over_budget_view_includes_all_nine_fields` and\n  `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`'s `_PASSTHROUGH_LEGACY_KEYS` assertion.\n- `summary` Field description: no test found referencing this string (\u00a74.4) \u2014 nothing to preserve.\n\n## 6. Test Design (AC7 \u2014 test files; AC3 adds one new assertion)\n\nUnit-level, calling the two builder functions directly, matching the existing direct-call\nconvention in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py`. Tests 6.1 and 6.2\nare unchanged from the prior spec (\u00a75.1, \u00a75.2); 6.3 adds the AC3 assertion; 6.4 is the\nrequest-level test from the prior spec (\u00a75.3), unchanged.\n\n### 6.1 `_build_compact_manifest()` ordering test (AC1)\n\n```python\ndef test_compact_manifest_hint_leads_with_section_narrowing() -> None:\n    \"\"\"_hint's first line must be a section-narrowing call, not summary=False (AC1).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture with a non-empty sections_index / sections dict>\n    manifest = _build_compact_manifest(result, full_response={}, selector=\"#3060\")\n    first_line = manifest[\"_hint\"].split(\"\\n\", 1)[0]\n    unqualified_full_load = \"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='#3060', summary=False)\"\n    assert \"section=\" in first_line\n    assert first_line != unqualified_full_load\n```\n\n### 6.2 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch unit test (AC2)\n\n```python\ndef test_over_budget_view_narrowed_usage_names_map_not_sections() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Narrowed-to-one-section _usage must not repeat sections= advice and must\n    name a resolvable mechanism (AC2).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture>\n    view = _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, full_chars=99999, selector=\"#3060\", narrowed_to_single_section=True\n    )\n    usage = view[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"sections=\" not in usage\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"#3060\" in usage\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\n### 6.3 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch budget-limit test (AC3, new this pass)\n\n```python\ndef test_over_budget_view_narrowed_usage_states_map_budget_limit() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Narrowed _usage must not present map=True as unconditionally safe (AC3).\"\"\"\n    result = <ViewItemResult fixture>\n    view = _build_over_budget_view(\n        result, full_chars=99999, selector=\"#3060\", narrowed_to_single_section=True\n    )\n    usage = view[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"over_budget\" in usage\n    assert \"no smaller retrieval\" in usage\n```\n\nThis pins the limit statement literally \u2014 matching the existing convention of pinning exact\nsubstrings elsewhere in this test file (\u00a76.2 above; the prior spec's \u00a73.4/\u00a74.4). If the\nimplementation's exact wording differs from \u00a75.4's, update this assertion to match the\nimplementation's actual chosen phrase, not the other way around: the requirement is that a\nlimit-stating sentence exists, not this specific string.\n\n### 6.4 `_build_over_budget_view()` narrowed-branch integration test (AC2/AC3 at the request level, required)\n\nAC2 and AC3 are phrased at the request level, and \u00a75.3's predicate is exactly what 6.1-6.3 cannot\nexercise (they bypass `backlog_view()` entirely). Drive the real tool end to end against a fixture\nitem with a section whose own content alone still exceeds `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`:\n\n```python\nasync def test_backlog_view_section_narrowed_over_budget_names_map(monkeypatch) -> None:\n    \"\"\"A section= request that narrows to one section and is still over budget must\n    receive a _usage naming map=True with its budget caveat, not repeated\n    sections=/section= advice (AC2, AC3).\"\"\"\n    response = await backlog_view(selector=\"<fixture selector>\", summary=False, section=\"<one oversized section>\")\n    assert response[\"_over_budget\"] is True\n    usage = response[\"_usage\"]\n    assert \"map=True\" in usage\n    assert \"over_budget\" in usage\n    assert \"sections=['\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='0\" not in usage\n    assert \"section='/\" not in usage\n```\n\nIf this test fails to observe `narrowed_to_single_section` evaluating `True` at the call site (no\n`map=True` in the response), \u00a75.3's predicate has a defect and must be corrected \u2014 do not adjust\nthe assertion to match incorrect behavior.\n\n### 6.5 Existing suite (AC7)\n\nNo changes required to any of the 5 files AC7 names beyond adding the tests above:\n`test_backlog_core_server.py`, `test_backlog_view_disclosure_tool.py`,\n`test_view_over_budget_gate.py`, `test_view_pagination_section_index.py`,\n`test_section_filter_miss.py`. Per \u00a75.6, every existing substring/field-name assertion in those\nfiles continues to pass unmodified \u2014 confirmed by direct diff review against `origin/main`, not\nmerely by the absence of an obvious break.\n\n## 7. AC8 \u2014 Landing on `origin/main`\n\n### 7.1 The problem\n\n`gh pr view 3649` (checked by the 2026-09-19 re-groom, not re-verified this pass \u2014 re-verify before\nacting on it) reports PR #3649 as `mergeable: CONFLICTING`, branch `fix/3060-r7-hints`, and the\nbranch touches all three `plugin.json` manifests (`.claude-plugin/`, `.codex-plugin/`,\n`.cursor-plugin/`) in addition to `backlog_core/server.py` and the three test files. Two problems\ncompound: the branch conflicts with current `main`, and \u2014 independent of the conflict \u2014 the\nbranch's `server.py` diff is incomplete against the final 8-criterion Acceptance Criteria (it has\nno AC3 or AC5 change, per the table in \u00a71).\n\n### 7.2 Resolution approach (Q4)\n\nDo not hand-resolve the `plugin.json` version-number hunks. This repo bumps that version via a\npre-commit hook (`plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md`: \"the version is in\n`.claude-plugin/plugin.json`, bumped by a pre-commit hook\") \u2014 a manually resolved conflict there is\nguessing at a value the tooling recomputes on the next commit regardless. Given the branch is\nalready both conflicting and incomplete, the lower-risk path is to treat PR #3649 as the reference\nfor AC1/AC2/AC4's already-correct `server.py` and test diffs (verified unchanged in \u00a72, \u00a75.1-5.3\nabove) and land the fix as a fresh branch off current `origin/main` tip, carrying:\n\n1. \u00a75.1-5.3 (AC1, AC2 mechanism \u2014 same content PR #3649 already drafted correctly).\n2. \u00a75.4 (AC2 wording plus the new AC3 sentence \u2014 PR #3649's version lacks the AC3 sentence).\n3. \u00a75.5 (AC5 \u2014 absent from PR #3649 entirely).\n4. \u00a76.1-6.4 tests (6.1/6.2/6.4 exist on PR #3649 in some form per the re-groom's PR-diff read; 6.3\n   is new).\n\nBefore starting: run `/rebase`'s pre-analysis (or read\n`plugins/development-harness/docs/branch-transfer-preflight.md` directly, per this repo's own\nbranch-transfer preflight rule) to get a per-file disposition rather than resolving conflicts by\nfeel \u2014 this is a process instruction for the implementing task, not an architectural decision this\ndocument makes on its behalf. Close or supersede PR #3649 once its content is reproduced on the new\nbranch, so the repository does not carry two open PRs claiming the same fix.\n\n### 7.3 What AC8 verifies, mechanically\n\n`git log origin/main --oneline | grep <merge commit>` (or equivalent) after the PR for the new\nbranch merges. Not satisfied by an open PR, a local branch, or a plan reporting 100% complete with\nnothing pushed \u2014 this is the exact failure mode AC8 exists to catch, per its own text and per this\nitem's Dependencies 8.2 provenance (plan `Pe6bcc5c2` previously reported complete with nothing\nmerged).\n\n## 8. Module Boundaries (unchanged \u2014 confirms Impact Radius Files table)\n\n| File | This fix | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| `backlog_core/server.py` | **Change target** | Both hint-construction functions, the call site, and the `summary` Field description live here |\n| `backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Read-only reference | `map=True`/`next_call` pattern already correct; not touched |\n| `backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Read-only reference | Consulted only to prove \u00a72's ordinal-compatibility finding |\n| `backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Read-only reference | Same as above |\n| `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` | Not touched | States R7; no wording change needed |\n| `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Not touched by this fix | AC6 requires #3060 to *stay* listed in the ownership table until AC1-5/7/8 hold \u2014 removing the row is a follow-up close-out action once the fix is verified merged, not part of this diff |\n\n## 9. ADRs\n\n### 9.1 `map=True`, never `navigate=<N>`, in the narrowed `_usage` (from prior spec, unchanged)\n\n**Decision**: `_build_over_budget_view()`'s narrowed-branch `_usage` names `map=True`, never\n`navigate=<N>`. **Context**: two independently implemented section-numbering schemes coexist in\n`backlog_core/` (\u00a72) and are proven non-equivalent under duplicate section titles.\n**Alternatives rejected**: giving `_build_over_budget_view()` its own `OrdinalPathMapper` build\n(duplicates `map=True`'s own code path, violates Anti-Goals 2/3); naming `navigate=` only when\nindependently verified resolvable (same cost, no benefit over calling `map=True` directly).\n**Consequence**: stays inside `server.py`, never asserts an address it cannot guarantee.\n\n### 9.2 Naming `over_budget`, not `#3059`, as the AC3 recourse (new this pass)\n\n**Decision**: the AC3 limit sentence names the MAP response's own `over_budget` field as the\nobservable follow-up check, not issue #3059 by number. **Context**: Q1 left this open; both\noptions satisfy AC3's literal text. **Alternatives rejected**: naming `#3059` \u2014 an issue number is\nnot something the calling agent can act on inside its own tool-call loop, and referencing unshipped\nwork risks reading as a second prohibition-1-shaped suggestion (\"wait\" is not an action available\nno